WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:13.510
RAKESH DHOOPAR: First of
all, I want to welcome you

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:16.480
to Google Next 2021.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.540
In this session, I
will focus on what role

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.480
integrated
observability tools play

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.570
in helping developing teams
become elite-performing teams.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:28.765
So without further
delay, let's get started.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.330
My name is Rakesh Dhoopar.

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:38.350
I'm a Director of Product
Management at Google.

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:41.920
I focus on our products
related to cloud observability.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:45.580
In today's session, I
will touch on five areas.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:48.040
First, I will start
by talking about,

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:53.000
what do we mean by the term,
"elite-performing teams?"

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.910
While transformation towards
becoming an elite team usually

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:59.210
requires a wide variety
of changes, including

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:02.480
adhering to certain
operational principles,

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:05.900
evolving operational
processes, and adopting

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.550
a variety of tools,
in this session,

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:13.400
I will focus on
observability tools only.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.150
We call our offering for
observability the Cloud Ops

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:19.460
Suite.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:21.050
I will then dive
deeper into some

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:23.750
of the specific capabilities
that help developers

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:26.360
and operators
reduce the time they

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:30.470
need to spend on identifying,
diagnosing, and troubleshooting

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:32.140
problems.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:33.730
Now, this part of
the talk will focus

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:36.490
on how we make it
easier to instrument

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:40.690
and collect telemetry signals
and analyze them effectively

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:43.950
to improve troubleshooting.

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:47.910
Lastly, I will talk about how
developers and operators can

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:50.340
overcome friction
between the two teams

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:53.745
so that they can move fast
while maintaining reliability

00:01:53.745 --> 00:01:54.495
of their services.

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:00.610
So let me first start
by defining what we mean

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:03.700
by the term, "elite teams."

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:07.600
And to illustrate that, I will
refer to a quote from the Dora

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:08.600
report.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:12.070
So some of you may be wondering,
what is the Dora report?

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:16.000
Now, at its very foundation Dora
is an ongoing research program

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.200
sponsored by Google.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.560
It is the largest and
longest-running research

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:22.800
program of its kind.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.080
The research program
collects and analyzes data

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:28.590
from over 30,000
practitioners and analyzes

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:31.440
the practices that drive
their software delivery

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.120
and operations performance.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.220
Participating
organizations are measured

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:38.840
on metrics for
software development,

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:41.660
metrics for software
deployment, and metrics

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:44.080
for service operations.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.000
From this collected
data, we then

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.970
do cluster analysis to
benchmark organizations

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:53.260
against the industry and
identify characteristics

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:57.340
that are grouped into cohorts
for low, medium, high,

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:00.200
and elite performers.

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:03.620
Elite teams are the highest
performers on at least one

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:05.990
of the metrics measured
for either software

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:08.660
development, software
deployment, or service

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:10.230
operations.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:13.160
Now, one of the most interesting
findings from this research

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:16.010
is that many professionals
approach these metrics

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:19.200
as representing a
set of trade-offs.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.500
Now, these professionals believe
that increasing throughput

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.480
of software development
will negatively

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:29.360
impact the availability and
reliability of their services.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:31.790
However, to the
contrary, this research

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:34.340
has consistently shown,
for the last six years,

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:38.840
that speed and stability are
outcome that enable each other.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:41.000
Simply stated,
elite performers are

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.330
able to move fast and
deploy their code rapidly

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:47.398
without compromising
on service reliability.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:53.100
The research also
found that elite teams

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:55.170
were able to deploy
multiple times

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:58.410
a day, while low performers
were deploying less than once

00:03:58.410 --> 00:03:59.370
per month.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:02.070
Now, at the same time,
these elite performers

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:05.660
had a time to restore a
service of less than one hour,

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:08.850
while the low performers
reported between one week

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:09.630
to one month.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:16.500
I'm going to briefly talk about
one of our customers in more

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:19.829
detail, and that
customer is Lowe's.

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:23.670
So Lowe's is one of the largest
do-it-yourself home improvement

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:26.230
retailer in the United States.

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:28.570
Now, as a part of their
digital transformation efforts,

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:31.330
they decided to modernize
some of their applications

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:33.160
using Google Cloud.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.530
Now, as they went through
this business transformation,

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:38.710
they worked closely with Google
to transform their organization

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:41.200
from a traditional
IT ops organization

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:44.680
to an SRE-type organization.

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:46.660
For their observability
tools, they

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:49.450
picked our cloud-native
capabilities offered

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:52.460
by the Cloud Operations Suite.

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:56.300
When they embarked on this
journey in 2019, in early 2020,

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:59.060
they had to accelerate
this transformation effort.

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:02.060
The pandemic forced
more and more customers

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:03.740
to transact online.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:06.470
And they had to integrate
these cloud-native apps

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:08.720
with pricing systems
that were running

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:12.680
on prem and with many store
best inventory systems

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:14.660
so that they could
fulfill customer orders

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:17.820
from a particular store
in a timely manner.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:20.870
Then, during the Black
Friday, Cyber Monday period,

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:23.870
their transaction volume
increased by 3 times

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:27.520
of their already
high daily volume.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:29.740
Lowe's handled the
entire workload

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:33.850
and their observability
challenges without any hiccups.

00:05:33.850 --> 00:05:37.420
The results they shared
with us were simply amazing.

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:39.340
They were able to
reduce their mean time

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:43.500
to acknowledge from 30 minutes
to 1 minute, their mean time

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:46.710
to restore a service from
2 hours to 20 minutes,

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:51.360
while increasing their
development velocity by 300x.

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:55.080
They were able to move
from one monolithic release

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:59.730
every two weeks to
20-plus releases a day.

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:02.450
Now, that's quite a story.

00:06:02.450 --> 00:06:05.980
So let's dive into the
observability tools offering

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:07.330
from Google.

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:10.840
Collectively, we call these
capabilities Cloud Operations

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:11.340
Suite.

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:17.980
Cloud Operations Suite is
Google's observability solution

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:20.260
that was designed for scale.

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:23.290
It includes Cloud
Monitoring, Cloud Logging,

00:06:23.290 --> 00:06:26.140
and advanced
observability tools.

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:28.270
Cloud Monitoring
captures and analyzes

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:30.580
system and application metrics.

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:33.340
It also enables users
to define and monitor

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:35.020
service-level objectives.

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:39.730
And it automatically tracks
corresponding error budgets.

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:43.090
Cloud Logging offers users the
ability to aggregate system

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:46.090
and application logs,
and explore these logs

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:50.540
with an intuitive
log explorer UI.

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:54.320
Our advanced observability
tools include Cloud Trace.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:58.670
And that enables users to track
how requests are propagating

00:06:58.670 --> 00:07:01.070
through your applications
or microservices

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:03.440
and receive detailed
real-time insights.

00:07:06.790 --> 00:07:08.950
Now, it is important to
highlight the Cloud Logging

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:12.130
and Monitoring are both
built on the same platform

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:17.100
that Google uses internally
for its SRE operations.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:19.760
Now, as a result, as
your applications scale,

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:21.340
you don't have to
worry about scaling

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:23.320
your observability tools.

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:26.710
Google Cloud Logging processes
about 2 and 1/2 exabytes

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:29.490
of log data every month.

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:31.530
Now, while most
enterprises may not

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:34.020
require that level
of scalability,

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:36.780
you should feel comfortable
that logging and monitoring

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:39.520
systems will be able
to scale not if,

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:42.210
but when your business
needs suddenly change

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:45.810
and you witness an order
of magnitude more users

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:50.270
or are faced with any kind
of cloud burst use cases.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:53.730
And that has actually happened
with several of our customers.

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:56.460
And I will share a
couple of examples here.

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:00.420
Gannett, a media company
that owns "USA Today,"

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:02.910
they saw an order of
magnitude higher number

00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:08.010
of users on election
night, 2020 in the US.

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:12.330
In another example, one
of our gaming customers

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:17.190
saw their users expand
overnight every time they

00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:19.800
launched new games.

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:23.670
So they have easily seen more
than a million users join over

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:25.560
a very short period of time.

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:27.450
And in both cases,
the customers were

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:29.130
able to scale
their applications,

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:30.990
and along with
those applications,

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:32.520
their observability solutions.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:37.830
Similarly, our
monitoring service

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:42.440
is also based on a highly
scalable time series platform.

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:44.660
When applications are
monolithic and deployed

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:47.000
on a small number
of named servers,

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:48.980
the volume of metrics
and monitoring tools

00:08:48.980 --> 00:08:50.700
was not very high.

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:53.490
Now, with the move to Kubernetes
and microservices-based

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:57.000
architectures, the number of
components generating metrics

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:59.240
has gone way up.

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:02.030
In some instances, we have
seen that metric volumes

00:09:02.030 --> 00:09:06.670
have actually gone up
by almost 100 times.

00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:09.910
Also, because of the ephemeral
nature of components,

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:11.980
the granularity of
metrics has shifted

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:14.050
from 1-minute
collection frequency

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:16.210
down to 10-second
collection frequency.

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:21.020
And sometimes, even down to
1-second collection frequency.

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:26.220
All of these have created
extremely high requirements

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:29.430
on scaling the underlying
time series platforms.

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:32.790
And we want to make sure that we
provide a platform that scales

00:09:32.790 --> 00:09:35.713
to meet your toughest
scaling requirements.

00:09:39.170 --> 00:09:40.900
So with that
high-level overview,

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:43.450
let us first look
at how users can

00:09:43.450 --> 00:09:45.550
instrument their
applications and collect

00:09:45.550 --> 00:09:48.400
telemetry signals from
systems and applications that

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:49.195
are running on GCP.

00:09:52.810 --> 00:09:55.210
Instrumentation and data
collection have always

00:09:55.210 --> 00:09:56.720
been a challenge in IT.

00:09:56.720 --> 00:09:59.590
And we have seen a proliferation
of agents over the past two

00:09:59.590 --> 00:10:01.180
decades.

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:04.060
Google has been a strong
proponent of open standards.

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:07.180
And we've been actively
driving OpenTelemetry standard

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:11.650
for service instrumentation
and signal collection.

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:14.470
Now, as the standards
evolve and mature,

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:16.690
we have been adopting
it in our products

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:18.850
to make it easier for
developers to instrument

00:10:18.850 --> 00:10:20.850
their applications.

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:24.750
The OpenTelemetry standard
consists of three key things.

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:28.590
A, language-specific API
and SDK for developers

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:30.810
to instrument
their applications.

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:33.840
B, the OpenTelemetry
collector and receivers

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:35.880
for collecting signals.

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:38.610
And C, the
OpenTelemetry exporter

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:41.610
for sending data to different
vendors for storage,

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:44.820
visualization, and alerting.

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:47.010
We're currently working on
instrumenting our client

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:51.150
libraries with OpenTelemetry,
starting with the gRPC library.

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:54.270
We believe that as the
OpenTelemetry standards mature

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:56.550
across all three
signal types, it

00:10:56.550 --> 00:10:58.680
will provide huge
benefits for developers

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:00.540
to start instrumenting
their applications

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:03.690
from the very beginning,
rather than wait for incidents

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:06.960
to happen and then have
the operations team define

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:11.170
what needs to be instrumented
in the application.

00:11:11.170 --> 00:11:13.630
Secondly, to provide
out-of-the-box support

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:18.130
for OpenTelemetry, we recently
released the unified ops agent.

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:19.930
The unified ops
agent makes it easier

00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:24.270
to install, configure, and
upgrade the collection agent.

00:11:24.270 --> 00:11:26.910
This unified agent combines
the monitoring and logging

00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:29.580
agent into one
installable component,

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:34.190
and provides a single YAML-based
configuration language.

00:11:34.190 --> 00:11:36.530
We're migrating our
third-party application

00:11:36.530 --> 00:11:38.930
stack monitoring
plugins to become

00:11:38.930 --> 00:11:40.790
OpenTelemetry-based receivers.

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:45.340
And those should be available
through the rest of this year.

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:48.100
Now, Prometheus is a very
popular open-source monitoring

00:11:48.100 --> 00:11:50.350
service in Kubernetes
environments.

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:53.680
Now, in the spirit of openness
and support for open source,

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:57.050
we support and integrate
with Prometheus in two ways.

00:11:57.050 --> 00:11:59.980
First, we have integrations
with the Prometheus engine

00:11:59.980 --> 00:12:03.610
to make it easier for users
to ingest Kubernetes metrics

00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:06.130
and workload metrics
from Prometheus to Cloud

00:12:06.130 --> 00:12:09.520
Monitoring and leverage the
rich capabilities in Cloud

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:10.210
Monitoring.

00:12:10.210 --> 00:12:13.810
That includes alerting,
dashboarding, and metric query

00:12:13.810 --> 00:12:15.270
language.

00:12:15.270 --> 00:12:18.750
Second, we recently announced
the Google-managed Prometheus

00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:22.050
service for users who want
to continue using Prometheus,

00:12:22.050 --> 00:12:24.420
but do not want to stand
up the infrastructure

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:26.610
behind Prometheus on
their own, and then

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:27.880
manage the Prometheus service.

00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:34.490
Now, for GKE environments
specifically, we

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:37.910
have made signal
collection even easier.

00:12:37.910 --> 00:12:39.710
One of the key
benefits of Cloud Ops

00:12:39.710 --> 00:12:41.660
is that users do
not have to deploy

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:43.790
any agents for
collecting metrics

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:46.490
and logs in GKE environments.

00:12:46.490 --> 00:12:50.000
Not only does this reduce
the toil of deploying agents,

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:52.400
this is very critical in
Kubernetes environments,

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:54.590
because the containers
are ephemeral.

00:12:54.590 --> 00:12:56.040
And in some cases,
the containers

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:59.060
may have very short lifespans,
as they are automatically

00:12:59.060 --> 00:13:03.220
scaled up and down
based on policies.

00:13:03.220 --> 00:13:05.650
This automatic collection
includes key metrics

00:13:05.650 --> 00:13:09.190
for a variety of GKE objects,
including nodes, pods,

00:13:09.190 --> 00:13:11.940
and clusters.

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:14.215
In addition to the
system logs, application

00:13:14.215 --> 00:13:15.840
logs that are written
with standard out

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:17.550
and standard error
are automatically

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:20.260
collected as well.

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:23.190
Now, more importantly,
the preconfigured agent

00:13:23.190 --> 00:13:25.620
automatically
captures metadata and

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:28.350
the hierarchical relationships
amongst all of the Kubernetes

00:13:28.350 --> 00:13:32.640
objects, from clusters to
nodes to services, all the way

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:35.400
to pods and containers.

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:37.650
As we will see later,
these relationships

00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:39.450
come in very handy
when users are

00:13:39.450 --> 00:13:42.930
exploring observability data
for diagnosing problems.

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:48.060
Let us now briefly look
at what capabilities

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:50.940
we provide for signal
analysis and visualization.

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:58.460
As part of the Cloud Ops Suite,
we offer an out-of-the-box GKE

00:13:58.460 --> 00:13:59.870
dashboard.

00:13:59.870 --> 00:14:03.560
The dashboard provides a very
rich and powerful experience

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:06.440
that was purpose-built for GKE.

00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:09.740
The dashboard aggregates
all metrics, logs,

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:13.640
service-level objectives,
Kubernetes events, and alerts

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:18.070
into one place for all
of your GKE clusters.

00:14:18.070 --> 00:14:20.410
Entities and their data,
presented in this dashboard,

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:23.470
are related to each other using
the context graph that I talked

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:25.960
about on the previous slide.

00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:28.480
When the context graph
is not visible in the UI,

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:31.750
when you start filtering
data in this dashboard,

00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:35.910
the presence of the context
graph becomes evident.

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:39.390
The filtering is not just a
text or label-based filtering,

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:41.040
but the filtering
in this dashboard

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:43.800
happens based on the
relationships in the context

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:44.740
graph.

00:14:44.740 --> 00:14:49.140
So for example, when users
filter for a specific cluster,

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:51.300
only objects that
belong to that cluster

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:53.250
will be shown in the dashboard.

00:14:53.250 --> 00:14:56.100
Each section of the
dashboard, from namespace

00:14:56.100 --> 00:14:59.430
to node to service, will
be filtered based on

00:14:59.430 --> 00:15:02.820
whether or not they belong
to the specific cluster.

00:15:02.820 --> 00:15:06.420
Similarly, when a user filters
based on the name of a pod,

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:09.390
again, all the objects that
are not related to this pod

00:15:09.390 --> 00:15:11.160
will be filtered out.

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:13.500
Only the cluster, the
namespace, the node,

00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:15.930
and the service that
this pod belongs to

00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:17.970
will be shown in the dashboard.

00:15:17.970 --> 00:15:19.680
Now, this is extremely powerful.

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:21.150
And a lot of work
has actually gone

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:22.980
into scaling this
kind of filtering

00:15:22.980 --> 00:15:26.190
and presenting all of this
data in an aggregated manner.

00:15:29.790 --> 00:15:32.250
Cloud Operations offers
purpose-built explorers

00:15:32.250 --> 00:15:35.400
for each of the three
different types of signals.

00:15:35.400 --> 00:15:38.100
Namely, metrics,
logs, and traces.

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:40.680
So for metrics, we offer
the Metrics Explorer

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:44.250
for analyzing time series
data for creating charts

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:47.610
as well as for adding
these charts to a dashboard

00:15:47.610 --> 00:15:50.490
For advanced metric analysis,
we offer the metric query

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:54.060
language, which provides a
powerful query-based mechanism

00:15:54.060 --> 00:15:57.030
to manipulate this
time series data.

00:15:57.030 --> 00:16:00.750
Using these queries, users
can compute ratios of metrics.

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:03.550
Users can perform
time-shift analysis.

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:06.210
Users can analyze
arbitrary percentiles.

00:16:06.210 --> 00:16:10.050
And users can evaluate arbitrary
mathematical expressions

00:16:10.050 --> 00:16:12.890
on this time series data.

00:16:12.890 --> 00:16:17.030
Similarly the Logs Explorer
was purpose-built for analyzing

00:16:17.030 --> 00:16:17.960
logs.

00:16:17.960 --> 00:16:20.900
The Logs Explorer provides
a histogram view that

00:16:20.900 --> 00:16:24.260
helps users analyze log trends.

00:16:24.260 --> 00:16:28.080
Writing log analysis
queries can be tedious.

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:32.360
So we've made it a lot easier to
create, save, and share queries

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:35.190
for analyzing these logs.

00:16:35.190 --> 00:16:37.620
For richer analysis
of log data, users

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:40.170
can extract and analyze
fields from log data,

00:16:40.170 --> 00:16:42.480
as well as define
streaming alerts

00:16:42.480 --> 00:16:45.170
on individual log events.

00:16:45.170 --> 00:16:47.630
Logs can also be
converted into metrics.

00:16:47.630 --> 00:16:50.480
And then, users can leverage
all of the capabilities

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:54.200
available in Cloud Monitoring,
which includes visualization,

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:57.620
learning, dashboarding,
or defining SLOs

00:16:57.620 --> 00:17:00.950
on these logs-based metrics.

00:17:00.950 --> 00:17:03.590
Lastly, the Trace Explorer
provides an interface

00:17:03.590 --> 00:17:07.670
that makes it easy to visualize
all traces and associated

00:17:07.670 --> 00:17:10.760
latencies, filter
traces of interest

00:17:10.760 --> 00:17:16.900
based on different criteria,
and define alerts on trace data

00:17:16.900 --> 00:17:20.560
so that users can do
behavioral comparison of how

00:17:20.560 --> 00:17:23.530
the latency of a specific
trace compares over time.

00:17:27.359 --> 00:17:30.750
While GKE Dashboard provides a
curated and tailored experience

00:17:30.750 --> 00:17:33.540
for GKE-based apps,
many customers

00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:38.010
still need to build
custom dashboards.

00:17:38.010 --> 00:17:40.560
We did quite an extensive
amount of UEX research

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:42.480
over the past year
to understand what

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:45.900
users need when they're actually
creating these dashboards.

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:49.230
Based on that research, we
have created a pretty intuitive

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:54.790
experience for creating and
editing custom dashboards.

00:17:54.790 --> 00:17:57.600
There's a variety
of new chart types,

00:17:57.600 --> 00:18:00.600
dynamically re-sizable
charts, easy way

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:03.900
to drag and drop
editing on a dashboard,

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:07.620
as well as use the full power
of the metrics query language

00:18:07.620 --> 00:18:12.090
within the dashboard itself
to explore the data before you

00:18:12.090 --> 00:18:14.065
add it to a dashboard.

00:18:14.065 --> 00:18:15.440
Now, one of the
interesting chart

00:18:15.440 --> 00:18:19.200
types we've added recently
is the Alert chart type.

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:21.810
When a user adds this
chart to the dashboard,

00:18:21.810 --> 00:18:25.170
they can also associate an
alert policy with that chart.

00:18:25.170 --> 00:18:27.510
And users can then see
the alert threshold

00:18:27.510 --> 00:18:30.650
from that policy on this chart.

00:18:30.650 --> 00:18:33.860
Finally, to automate the
creation of dashboards

00:18:33.860 --> 00:18:36.260
we have populated
a GitHub repository

00:18:36.260 --> 00:18:39.470
with over 60
dashboard templates,

00:18:39.470 --> 00:18:42.680
and a Terraform module to
automate the deployment

00:18:42.680 --> 00:18:44.740
of these templates.

00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:48.730
In addition, we recently added
a sample dashboard capability

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:51.370
directly into the
product UI that

00:18:51.370 --> 00:18:54.700
makes it easy to ingest and
adapt these sample dashboards

00:18:54.700 --> 00:18:57.770
from the GitHub repository.

00:18:57.770 --> 00:19:00.320
Users can get a preview of
each of these dashboards

00:19:00.320 --> 00:19:04.580
without any effort before they
start adapting and adopting

00:19:04.580 --> 00:19:06.345
these sample dashboards.

00:19:09.200 --> 00:19:11.870
Let us finally look at
how development teams can

00:19:11.870 --> 00:19:13.750
become elite-performing teams.

00:19:13.750 --> 00:19:16.250
And the most important thing
we will discuss in this section

00:19:16.250 --> 00:19:19.640
is, how the two teams can
collaborate amongst each other.

00:19:23.560 --> 00:19:26.160
Well, the collaboration between
development and ops teams

00:19:26.160 --> 00:19:28.200
can be improved by
defining and measuring

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:29.490
service-level objectives.

00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:32.160
That is easier said than done.

00:19:32.160 --> 00:19:34.080
To improve that
collaboration, we

00:19:34.080 --> 00:19:36.120
recommend that both
developers and operators

00:19:36.120 --> 00:19:39.210
have a seat at the design table
early on in the software design

00:19:39.210 --> 00:19:42.990
process so they can agree
to a common language,

00:19:42.990 --> 00:19:45.990
agree on a best way to
measure reliability,

00:19:45.990 --> 00:19:49.680
and create reliability as a
shared objective for the two

00:19:49.680 --> 00:19:51.180
teams.

00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:54.070
Now, once they have agreed to
the service-level indicators

00:19:54.070 --> 00:19:55.500
and what service-level
objectives

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:59.480
they want to achieve, they can
then use these capabilities

00:19:59.480 --> 00:20:04.020
within the Service Monitoring
feature in Cloud Operations.

00:20:04.020 --> 00:20:05.850
Cloud Monitoring-- or
Service Monitoring--

00:20:05.850 --> 00:20:07.980
will automatically
and continuously

00:20:07.980 --> 00:20:09.960
keep track of how
the teams are doing

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:13.060
with respect to the agreed SLO.

00:20:13.060 --> 00:20:15.310
The error budget
is automatically

00:20:15.310 --> 00:20:17.890
computed and tracked as well.

00:20:17.890 --> 00:20:20.710
As long as the error budget
for a given time period

00:20:20.710 --> 00:20:23.140
has not been
exhausted, developers

00:20:23.140 --> 00:20:25.540
can move with full
velocity to continue

00:20:25.540 --> 00:20:27.650
pushing out new capabilities.

00:20:27.650 --> 00:20:31.210
However, as the error budget
gets close to exhaustion,

00:20:31.210 --> 00:20:33.970
the mechanism becomes
self-regulating.

00:20:33.970 --> 00:20:36.400
New features are slowed
down, and both developers

00:20:36.400 --> 00:20:39.430
and operator shifts towards
improving the service

00:20:39.430 --> 00:20:41.730
reliability.

00:20:41.730 --> 00:20:43.700
Now, as organizations
improve the collaboration

00:20:43.700 --> 00:20:46.670
between developers and
operators around SLOs,

00:20:46.670 --> 00:20:49.160
they also need to adopt
some best practices

00:20:49.160 --> 00:20:51.000
that we have shared.

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:54.300
These best practices
include A, accepting

00:20:54.300 --> 00:21:00.600
that failure is normal and
not striving towards 100% SLO.

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:05.890
B, measuring everything,
including toil and reliability,

00:21:05.890 --> 00:21:09.280
and using automation and
observability tools to reduce

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:12.280
toil so developers and
operators can contribute

00:21:12.280 --> 00:21:15.300
towards feature velocity.

00:21:15.300 --> 00:21:17.850
Lastly, when an
incident does happen,

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:19.830
we recommend that
the two teams strive

00:21:19.830 --> 00:21:23.130
towards blameless
postmortems and drive service

00:21:23.130 --> 00:21:24.928
reliability improvements.

00:21:28.760 --> 00:21:31.190
Let us look at how
customers can use service

00:21:31.190 --> 00:21:32.990
monitoring to
adopt and implement

00:21:32.990 --> 00:21:35.530
some of these SRE principles.

00:21:35.530 --> 00:21:37.030
The service monitoring
capabilities

00:21:37.030 --> 00:21:39.820
enables users to select
a service-level indicator

00:21:39.820 --> 00:21:43.040
and define the
service-level objectives.

00:21:43.040 --> 00:21:45.820
Now, once the SLO is
defined, Cloud Monitoring

00:21:45.820 --> 00:21:49.840
will automatically compute
and track error budgets.

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:52.690
Users can also define alerts
on these error budgets.

00:21:52.690 --> 00:21:56.530
So as you reach a critical
threshold on your error budget,

00:21:56.530 --> 00:21:58.180
alerts will be generated.

00:21:58.180 --> 00:22:01.230
And users will be notified about
the exhaustion of the error

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:03.540
budget.

00:22:03.540 --> 00:22:06.450
For GKE services, there
is further out-of-the-box

00:22:06.450 --> 00:22:09.780
integration that makes it easier
to define the service-level

00:22:09.780 --> 00:22:11.070
objectives.

00:22:11.070 --> 00:22:12.960
Cloud Ops will
automatically discover

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:18.270
services that are using Istio
and ASM deployed on GKE.

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:21.480
So when a user defines an
SLO for these auto-discovered

00:22:21.480 --> 00:22:25.620
services, if they choose an
availability or latency metric,

00:22:25.620 --> 00:22:30.770
Cloud Ops will automatically
pick or recommend the metric

00:22:30.770 --> 00:22:34.110
to be used as a
service-level indicator.

00:22:34.110 --> 00:22:37.170
Now, since some customers
choose Prometheus to monitor

00:22:37.170 --> 00:22:38.820
Kubernetes services--

00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:41.310
and specifically, their
apps running in Kubernetes--

00:22:41.310 --> 00:22:44.580
we also provide out-of-the-box
integration for such customers

00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:47.190
to ingest metrics from
Prometheus and be able

00:22:47.190 --> 00:22:51.270
to define SLIs and SLOs
on Prometheus metrics.

00:22:51.270 --> 00:22:54.300
Of course, users can
define custom services

00:22:54.300 --> 00:22:59.430
or choose any other custom
metric to use as an SLI,

00:22:59.430 --> 00:23:02.010
and then define an
SLO on that metric.

00:23:05.630 --> 00:23:08.810
So let me summarize some of the
key highlights from this talk.

00:23:08.810 --> 00:23:12.900
First, what we have seen from
the analysis in the Dora report

00:23:12.900 --> 00:23:15.980
and from the customer
example I talked about.

00:23:15.980 --> 00:23:18.560
Elite teams can achieve
high feature velocity

00:23:18.560 --> 00:23:22.460
without sacrificing
service reliability.

00:23:22.460 --> 00:23:25.550
Two, from a tools point
of view, Cloud Operations

00:23:25.550 --> 00:23:28.880
offers integrated capabilities
that brings together the three

00:23:28.880 --> 00:23:30.950
different signals
to make it easier

00:23:30.950 --> 00:23:34.790
to improve MTGR and
enable developers and SRE

00:23:34.790 --> 00:23:38.710
users to focus on
increasing code velocity.

00:23:38.710 --> 00:23:41.980
Three, elite teams
use SLIs and SLOs

00:23:41.980 --> 00:23:45.190
as a mechanism to formalize
reliability targets

00:23:45.190 --> 00:23:46.960
between developers
and operators.

00:23:46.960 --> 00:23:49.570
And customers can use
these capabilities

00:23:49.570 --> 00:23:52.370
within Cloud Operations.

00:23:52.370 --> 00:23:56.330
If you're interested in more
sessions around this topic

00:23:56.330 --> 00:23:59.810
or interested in learning about
our Cloud Operations Suite,

00:23:59.810 --> 00:24:03.560
there are three additional
sessions listed on this slide.

00:24:03.560 --> 00:24:07.560
And I welcome you
to go watch those.

00:24:07.560 --> 00:24:10.200
Please enjoy those
sessions and other sessions

00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:12.630
in the Next 2021.

00:24:12.630 --> 00:24:16.100
And thank you again for
attending this session.

